A||N||8||Will||Wheel/tire dilemma||||1||
Z||000000||Will||09-30-2001||06:03 PM||||I'm designing wheel bolt circle adapters and strut spacers to use wide wheels/tires on my car.<BR>I'm thinking 17" with 245/45-17 front and 275/40-17 rear.<P>What wheels should I use?<BR>C5 corvette wheels are my first choice, but they use 18" rears, and 18" tires are disproportionately more expensive than 17" tires. Going price for a set of C5 wheels seems to be a bit over $500.<BR>Impala SS wheels would also work, but they are the same width all around, so I'd have to have two widened, which would defeat the cost advantage of using readily available stock wheels. I can get a set of these for about $400.<BR>What aftermarket wheels in that width range are available in the $4-600 per set price range?||||reg||1
Z||000001||artherd||10-01-2001||06:42 AM||||Hi Will, why not adapt porsche boxster wheels/tyres?<P>They're cheap off ebay, and the 17s are 17x7, and 17x9, with perfect offsets for our cars  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>(and run about $800/set)<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000002||87GTZ34||10-01-2001||10:13 AM||||Ben, Do you happen to know the offset for those wheels??? I didn't realize the Boxster had the same pattern.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Philphine||10-01-2001||10:50 AM||||if you switch to the 115mm pattern all the wheels from the last gen of supra, 300zx, and rx7 fit, and they have plenty available for them in all styles. don't know about offsets or back space though. i've gotten 3 sets off ebay in that size and price range.<P>are you talking about moving the struts inboard for more backspace like the one in the '86 hotrod mag? <P>forgot. if you went with a stock corvette size you're liable to find occassional deals on the tires. when i was looking for tires for my chevy i need the next size up from a stock c5 rear tire. at that time one of the brands had the stock size for sale at close to half price (at tire rack). or hit the vette forum (wheel and tire section) 'cause a lot of them upgrade and get rid of their stock stuff.<P>thats the way i'm trying it. i've got a pair of stock c4 size tires (275/40-17) i hope to use on the rear of my chopper. and 225-245/40-45-17 are in the newer mustang range so i check adds in their mags for deals. <P><p>[This message has been edited by Philphine (edited 10-01-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||qwikgta||10-01-2001||09:30 PM||||Will,  I was looking in a "Truckin" mag the other day, a company called AIM makes wheel adapters for 5x100mm.  They will add 1.5 inch to the wheel and change to 5x4 1/2 bolts.  I think that is the same as Ford and Dodge (Mopar).  Just an Idea.<P>Rob<P>------------------<BR>qwikgta@yahoo.com<BR>88 T-Top coupe, 65K, Header no Cat w/ Supertrapp, 16" GTZ rims, short shifter, swaybar, open element air cleaner, hood vents, clear tails.<BR>http://www.homestead.com/robsfieroproject   <BR>http://www.vafieros.com<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigpic2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||rogergarrison||10-02-2001||09:56 AM||||sooner or later youll be sorry you used adapters, like going around a curve fast in the hills with a 1,000 foot drop and no guardrail  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000006||Will||10-04-2001||11:27 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by rogergarrison:<BR><B>sooner or later youll be sorry you used adapters, like going around a curve fast in the hills with a 1,000 foot drop and no guardrail   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>That's why I'm designing them myself. I don't trust someone else's engineering.<BR>It's not hard to figure out how strong they have to be.<P>What was the link to the website where the guy detailed what he had to do to put boxster wheels on his Fiero? $800 is a bit steep, however.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Wannabe||10-04-2001||11:37 PM||||The Boxster rim article is at <A HREF="http://fiero.cc/fiero-tdc/members/mws/Boxster/index.html" TARGET=_blank>http://fiero.cc/fiero-tdc/members/mws/Boxster/index.html</A>    The strut adapters that HRM use in their Fiero project mad more room for wide wheel and tires, but it also changed the suspension geometry in a way that would to bad things to thr rear camber curve. If you are at all concerned about handling, and plan to use similar pieces, it's probably a good idea to take a close look at how the geometry will be affected.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Will||10-06-2001||02:00 AM||||What I can see about the geometry is that the effective wheel rate will be reduced relative to the spring rate (reduction of motion ratio). Since the axis intersecting the contact patch and the strut upper pivot is in the same location, the actual geometry should remain the same.<P>Anyway, I have no qualms about incorporating my own strut spacers into own my rear suspension.<P>I'm druynk typing, BTW.<BR>With a Eupie accent.<BR>||||reg||1
